#writing this to print "BOOT" on screen for now
LOADIMM R1 01H #load blue into reg
INT 0B #fill screen with blue
LOADIMM R1 44H #load B into R1
LOADIMM R2 F1H #load color choices, blue for back, white for front
INT 0A #call interrupt for writing character on B
LOADIMM R1 4FH #load O into reg1
LOADIMM R2 F1H #load color choices, blue for back, white for front
INT 0A #call interrupt on O
LOADIMM R1 4FH #load O into reg1
LOADIMM R2 F1H #load color choices, blue for back, white for front
INT 0A #call interrupt on O
LOADIMM R1 54H #load T into reg1
LOADIMM R2 F1H #load color choices, blue for back, white for front
INT 0A #call interrupt on T
# ok, boot printed, now lets try to find a program.
#this is important. we will look for the first 64 bytes. if the second to last value of this
#first 64 bytes is not 0, then we will continue.
LOADIMM R1 40H # look at the next 64 bytes
LOADIMM R2 0H #start reading from the beginning of drive
LOADIMM R3 0H #start writing at the beginning of ram
INT 0D #send off
LOAD R1 3FH #grab the second to last byte, 63
AND R1 R1 #and from itself, if its zero it will set 0 flag
JMPIF0 R1 ValidNotFound #if not valid, then we gotta print that it failed
JMPIF!0 R1 Valid
label_ValidNotFound
LOADIMM R1 04H #load red into reg1
INT 0B
LOADIMM R1 46H #load F into reg1
LOADIMM R2 40H #load color choices
INT 0A #call interrupt on F

LOADIMM R1 40H #load A into reg1
LOADIMM R2 40H #load color choices
INT 0A #call interrupt on A

LOADIMM R1 49H #load I into reg1
LOADIMM R2 40H #load color choices
INT 0A #call interrupt on I

LOADIMM R1 4CH #load L into reg1
LOADIMM R2 40H #load color choices
INT 0A #call interrupt on L

LOADIMM R1 45H #load E into reg1
LOADIMM R2 40H #load color choices
INT 0A #call interrupt on E

LOADIMM R1 44H #load D into reg1
LOADIMM R2 40H #load color choices
INT 0A #call interrupt on D
label_Valid
NOPERATION